00:00:07.334 --> 00:00:09.500
ENO COMPTON: You might have
heard about Google Cloud

00:00:09.500 --> 00:00:12.190
Functions, which makes
it possible to write

00:00:12.190 --> 00:00:15.580
simple, single-purpose code that
scales automatically and runs

00:00:15.580 --> 00:00:17.290
in response to events.

00:00:17.290 --> 00:00:20.069
You might also be wondering,
does it work with Go?

00:00:20.069 --> 00:00:22.360
ANGELA FUNK: Cloud Functions
recently announced support

00:00:22.360 --> 00:00:24.580
for Python 3.7 and Node 8.

00:00:24.580 --> 00:00:27.940
And today, we're excited to
show you the newly released Go

00:00:27.940 --> 00:00:30.100
support for Cloud Functions.

00:00:30.100 --> 00:00:31.360
Let's get started.

00:00:31.360 --> 00:00:34.290
ENO COMPTON: Go Cloud Functions
come in two distinct types--

00:00:34.290 --> 00:00:37.400
HTTP functions and
background functions.

00:00:37.400 --> 00:00:40.380
Let's take a look at
an HTTP function first.

00:00:40.380 --> 00:00:42.910
HTTP functions run when
they receive a request

00:00:42.910 --> 00:00:44.690
and they return a response.

00:00:44.690 --> 00:00:48.610
The function signature follows
the HTTP handler func interface

00:00:48.610 --> 00:00:50.650
from the Go standard
library, meaning

00:00:50.650 --> 00:00:53.890
that you can use the wide
variety of Go HTTP middleware

00:00:53.890 --> 00:00:55.540
right out of the box.

00:00:55.540 --> 00:00:57.040
ANGELA FUNK: Now
let's deploy it.

00:00:57.040 --> 00:00:59.140
We give our function
a name, hello,

00:00:59.140 --> 00:01:03.670
specify the entry point, F,
and add the trigger HTTP flag.

00:01:03.670 --> 00:01:05.260
Once it's deployed,
your command line

00:01:05.260 --> 00:01:07.960
will show the URL used
to trigger the function.

00:01:07.960 --> 00:01:10.270
Just copy that in your
browser to test it.

00:01:10.270 --> 00:01:12.910
And with that, we've just
deployed our first Go Cloud

00:01:12.910 --> 00:01:14.050
Function.

00:01:14.050 --> 00:01:17.050
ENO COMPTON: Next, let's take a
look at a background function.

00:01:17.050 --> 00:01:19.930
While an HTTP function
responds to a request,

00:01:19.930 --> 00:01:22.480
a background function
responds to an event.

00:01:22.480 --> 00:01:23.980
Let's write a
function that triggers

00:01:23.980 --> 00:01:27.310
every time a cloud storage
bucket's contents change.

00:01:27.310 --> 00:01:29.140
Here's what our
function looks like.

00:01:29.140 --> 00:01:31.120
It receives a
cloud storage event

00:01:31.120 --> 00:01:32.806
and logs the file
in bucket name.

00:01:32.806 --> 00:01:34.180
ANGELA FUNK:
Deployments function

00:01:34.180 --> 00:01:37.150
is just like deploying
an HTTP function.

00:01:37.150 --> 00:01:39.880
We give our function a name,
specify the entry point,

00:01:39.880 --> 00:01:42.400
and specify the event
type and resource

00:01:42.400 --> 00:01:44.290
that originates the event.

00:01:44.290 --> 00:01:46.630
Now with all the setup
complete, this function

00:01:46.630 --> 00:01:49.832
will fire every time a new
file is added to our bucket.

00:01:49.832 --> 00:01:51.790
Don't forget to create
the cloud storage bucket

00:01:51.790 --> 00:01:53.800
before deploying your function.

00:01:53.800 --> 00:01:55.750
Now, to test this,
just use the web UI

00:01:55.750 --> 00:01:57.710
and upload a file
to your bucket.

00:01:57.710 --> 00:01:59.710
ENO COMPTON: We've only
shown a very small piece

00:01:59.710 --> 00:02:01.991
of what's possible with
Go Cloud Functions.

00:02:01.991 --> 00:02:03.490
With Cloud Functions,
you don't have

00:02:03.490 --> 00:02:05.140
to worry about
server management.

00:02:05.140 --> 00:02:07.240
You only pay for what you
use, and your functions

00:02:07.240 --> 00:02:10.090
scale automatically
in response to demand.

00:02:10.090 --> 00:02:12.130
Best of all, you're using
the familiar building

00:02:12.130 --> 00:02:14.087
blocks of Go functions.

00:02:14.087 --> 00:02:15.670
ANGELA FUNK: Using
Go Cloud Functions,

00:02:15.670 --> 00:02:19.150
you can build mobile or IoT
back ends, virtual assistants

00:02:19.150 --> 00:02:22.060
or chat bots, do
real-time file processing,

00:02:22.060 --> 00:02:26.050
or integrate with third-party
services, just to name a few.

00:02:26.050 --> 00:02:28.600
What's best, though, is
that it's incredibly easy

00:02:28.600 --> 00:02:31.060
to get started by writing
a small function in Go

00:02:31.060 --> 00:02:32.960
and then building
it up from there.

00:02:32.960 --> 00:02:35.251
ENO COMPTON: We hope you give
Go Cloud functions a try.

00:02:35.251 --> 00:02:35.990
I'm Eno Compton.

00:02:35.990 --> 00:02:37.200
ANGELA FUNK: And I'm Angela.

